 ### [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md)
-The Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass renders scenes by marching rays through a neural Signed Distance Function (SDF) and accumulating color, normals, depth, and opacity along each ray. It computes the composite RGB image, surface normals, depth, and opacity by querying a geometry network for SDF values and gradients, computing opacity from the SDF and ray direction, and accumulating results along the ray.
+Renders scenes by marching rays through a neural SDF and accumulating color, normals, depth, and opacity. It computes composite RGB images by querying the geometry network for SDF values and gradients, setting itself apart by focusing on full photometric rendering with normal-based edge detection.
 
 ### [Differentiable Silhouette Rendering](4322-differentiable-silhouette-rendering.md)
-Differentiable Silhouette Rendering is a technique to render binary silhouettes of 3D shapes represented by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) in a way that allows gradient back-propagation to scene parameters. It solves the non-differentiability of standard binary silhouette detection (e.g., ray termination at infinity) by computing a "soft" silhouette value based on the minimum absolute SDF encountered along a ray. It sets itself apart from its parent [Differentiable Sphere Tracing](2316-differentiable-sphere-tracing.md) by focusing specifically on the silhouette supervision signal rather than full photometric or geometric rendering, and from siblings like [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) by relying on the minimum absolute distance query rather than normal-based edge detection.
+Renders binary silhouettes of 3D shapes represented by SDFs by computing a "soft" silhouette value based on the minimum absolute SDF encountered along a ray. It sets itself apart by focusing specifically on silhouette supervision rather than full photometric rendering.
 
 ### [SDF Direct Diffuse Integrator](4467-sdf-direct-diffuse-integrator.md)
-The SDF Direct Diffuse Integrator is a differentiable rendering method that computes direct illumination for scenes defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs). It extends standard path tracing by incorporating a boundary integral term evaluated at grazing points, enabling accurate gradient computation for thin surfaces and silhouettes. Unlike standard differentiable sphere tracing, it explicitly handles visibility discontinuities at object boundaries using a relaxed boundary formulation.
+Computes direct illumination for SDF scenes by incorporating a boundary integral term evaluated at grazing points. It sets itself apart by explicitly handling visibility discontinuities at object boundaries using a relaxed boundary formulation.
 
 ### [Single-View Depth Shape Completion](5426-single-view-depth-shape-completion.md)
-Recovers a complete 3D shape from a single depth image by optimizing a latent code through a differentiable renderer, requiring no additional training of the shape generator. It sets itself apart from its sibling [DeepSDF](link) by replacing fixed-distance sampling with adaptive sampling via differentiable sphere tracing, which better captures boundaries and thin structures. It differs from [Differentiable Sphere Tracing](2316-differentiable-sphere-tracing.md) by explicitly combining a Directional Distance Function (DDF) for ray intersection with a Signed Distance Function (SDF) for loss computation, avoiding standard sphere tracing iterations.
+Recovers 3D shape from a single depth image by optimizing a latent code. It sets itself apart by combining a Directional Distance Function (DDF) for ray intersection with an SDF for loss computation, avoiding standard sphere tracing iterations.
 
 ### [Multi-View Photometric Shape Prediction](5427-multi-view-photometric-shape-prediction.md)
-Predicts 3D shape from multiple color images by minimizing cross-view photometric consistency loss without requiring segmentation masks. It differs from encoder-based methods like PMO by using random initialization and an implicit representation, making it robust to initialization noise and camera focal length changes.
+Predicts 3D shape from multiple color images by minimizing cross-view photometric consistency loss. It sets itself apart by optimizing a latent code for a specific instance rather than training a network to predict vertex coordinates.
 
 ### [Camera Pose Optimization via Depth and Silhouette](5667-camera-pose-optimization-via-depth-and-silhouette.md)
-This method optimizes an initial camera pose toward a ground truth pose by minimizing gradients computed from 2D depth and binary silhouette observations rendered via a differentiable SDF renderer. It is specifically designed for scenarios where the 3D shape is known (fixed) but the camera parameters are uncertain. Unlike [Differentiable Sphere Tracing](2316-differentiable-sphere-tracing.md) which focuses on general scene rendering, this method isolates pose estimation as the primary optimization target, using image-based errors to drive gradient descent. It sets itself apart from [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) by focusing on pose recovery from perturbed initial views rather than shape reconstruction from a single view, and from [Differentiable Silhouette Rendering](4322-differentiable-silhouette-rendering.md) by explicitly combining silhouette cross-entropy with scale-invariant depth loss.
+Optimizes camera pose by minimizing gradients from 2D depth and binary silhouette observations. It sets itself apart by keeping the shape fixed and updating only the camera pose.
 
 ### [Reparameterization for Differentiable SDF Rendering](7868-reparameterization-for-differentiable-sdf-rendering.md)
-A differentiable rendering method for neural Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) that computes correct gradients with respect to geometric scene parameters (e.g., neural network weights) by reparameterizing the pixel integral to handle primary visibility discontinuities (silhouettes/occlusions) without requiring volumetric approximations or external geometric supervision like segmentation masks. It sets itself apart from prior SDF renderers (e.g., IDR [Yariv 2020]) by eliminating the need for per-view masks and volumetric approximations, and from mesh-based differentiable renderers by deriving a continuous warp function specifically for implicit SDF boundaries where direct sampling is challenging.
+Computes correct gradients w.r.t geometric scene parameters by reparameterizing the pixel integral to handle primary visibility discontinuities. It sets itself apart by eliminating the need for per-view masks and volumetric approximations.
 
 ### [Forward Rendering Pipeline with Closed-Form SG Integration](19274-forward-rendering-pipeline-with-closed-form-sg-integration.md)
-This method performs differentiable forward rendering of scenes defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) and Spherical Gaussian (SG) approximations for appearance. It solves the rendering equation by representing both the environment lighting and the Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) as SGs, allowing the integral to be computed in closed form. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Instant-NSR-PL Forward Pass](2540-instant-nsr-pl-forward-pass.md) by explicitly modeling the specular component via SGs for relighting, rather than relying solely on neural radiance fields or implicit neural representations without explicit physics-based material parameters.
+Performs differentiable forward rendering of SDF scenes with Spherical Gaussian (SG) approximations for appearance. It sets itself apart by explicitly modeling the specular component via SGs for relighting.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-- Time complexity scales with the number of pixels, the maximum number of marching iterations, and the computational cost of the neural network forward pass per query [S1].
-- Memory complexity is dominated by storing the ray positions, directions, and intermediate SDF values for all pixels, as well as the computational graph for back-propagation [S1].
